*Application for Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Electrical Machines, SAE-031274*

I am writing to apply for the *Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Electrical Machines* post (*SAE-031274*) in the Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering at the *University of Manchester*. I am currently a Research Assistant at the *Power Electronics and Machines Centre (PEMC), University of Nottingham*, having completed my *PhD in Electrical Engineering at Southeast University (SEU), Nanjing*, in September 2025 with the dissertation _Multi-physics Analysis and Design of Hairpin Winding PM Traction Machines in Electric Vehicles_. My research has focused on the electromagnetic design, thermal management, multi-physics optimisation and control of high-power-density electrical machines, and I would be glad to contribute to the Department's research and teaching in this area.

My research record to date includes *22 journal papers, of which 17 are in IEEE Transactions* (across _IEEE Trans. Industrial Electronics_, _Transportation Electrification_, _Industry Applications_, and _Energy Conversion_), 18 peer-reviewed conference papers at IEEE IEMDC, IEEE ECCE, ICEM and ICEMS, and 9 patents. The work covers novel _n_-layer flat-wire and hairpin winding design, electromagnetic analysis of flux-modulation and hybrid-excitation machines, high-fidelity thermal-network and oil-cooling modelling, multi-objective multi-physics optimisation, and converter-machine interaction under PWM excitation. This work has been recognised by the *2025 IEEE TEC Electric Vehicle Design Competition (1#super[st] Prize)*, the *2025 IEEE Trans. Transportation Electrification Prize Paper Award (2#super[nd] Prize)*, the *1#super[st] Prize in the IEEE IAS CMD Thesis Contest (non-PhD category)*, the *CIEEC Best Paper Award (2023)*, and the *Jiangsu Province Outstanding Master Degree Thesis Award (2022)*. I also serve as Co-Organiser and Co-Chair of an ICEM 2026 Special Session on _Next-Generation Electrified Transport_.

I have also gained substantive industrial-development experience through a collaboration with *Inovance*, a leading EV traction-machine supplier, on two oil-cooled PM traction machines, an *18 000 rpm 113 kW A0-class machine* (138 Nm, 7.78 kW/kg, 95.3% peak efficiency) and a *20 000 rpm 270 kW A-class machine* (380 Nm, 7.87 kW/kg, 97.4% peak efficiency). Each was taken through a full industrial cycle of multi-physics design (electromagnetic FEA, lumped-parameter thermal-network modelling and mechanical-stress evaluation), engineering drawings and tolerance specification for high-reliability manufacture, and AVL dynamometer characterisation. Alongside this, I have developed an *in-house FEA code package* covering *electromagnetic, structural-modal and mechanical-stress analyses*, now being applied to magnet-free synchronous-machine work at PEMC. This combined academic and industrial background should support applications for *EPSRC*, *Innovate UK* and *Aerospace Technology Institute (ATI)* funding, as well as direct industrial co-funded R&D with EV and aerospace machine manufacturers.

I would be happy to contribute to teaching across the *Electrical Machines and Drives curriculum* at undergraduate and MSc level, including *electromagnetic machine theory, finite-element principles, FEA-based machine design, thermal management, and machine control*, as well as converter-machine interaction. I would also be glad to develop new teaching content on contemporary traction-machine technology such as *hairpin winding and advanced cooling*, which is of direct relevance to students pursuing careers in EV and aerospace electrification. During my doctoral studies I served as *teaching assistant* on the SEU course _Integrated Practice of Electrical Machine Systems_, preparing tutorial material, leading Q&A, reviewing project proposals, and contributing mark recommendations. My toolchain spans MATLAB/Simulink, ANSYS Maxwell and Mechanical, Python, C/C++ for TI C2000 and STM32, and FPGA, which should be useful for project-based teaching and capstone supervision.

Although cryogenic machine design has not been my primary research focus, my thermal-modelling background, including *high-fidelity lumped-parameter networks and coupled fluid-thermal analyses*, provides a foundation that I would be glad to extend alongside Manchester's *superconducting and HTS-machine activities*. For laboratory health and safety, I have worked in *high-voltage electrical-machine test facilities at SEU and PEMC under formal H&S frameworks*, including risk assessment, laboratory induction and safe working procedures for rotating machinery and power-electronics test rigs, and I appreciate the responsibility of managing such environments for student use. My research also has a substantial power-electronics component, addressing *PWM-excitation effects on machine losses, field-winding voltage modulation, and induced-voltage pulsation*, and I have designed and built a *three-phase full-bridge converter with associated control* for flux-modulation machine testing, which provides a basis for contributing to power-electronics and AC power-circuit analysis teaching.

My CV and detailed responses to the person specification are attached. I would be glad to discuss my research and teaching plans further at interview.

Yours sincerely,

